   "t": "Спасибо за завтрак.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Slowly, around the breakfast table.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "mama",
   "t": "Доброе утро, Лёва.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Good morning, Lyova.' 'Utro' is a neuter word, so 'dobroe', good, ends in 'oe' to match.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Доброе утро.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Good morning.' Lyova hands the same two words straight back.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "mama",
   "t": "Виктор приготовил такой омлет.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Viktor made such an omelet.' 'Takoy' is 'such a', in the masculine to match 'omlet', a word Russian took from French. 'Prigotovil' is 'made, cooked'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Это типа извинения?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Is this a kind of apology?' 'Tipa' comes from 'tip', type, and is everyday slang for 'sort of'. 'Izvinenie', apology, shows up here as 'izvineniya'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Это типа омлет.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'This is a kind of omelet.' Barinov throws 'tipa' back but leaves 'omlet' unchanged. In slang, 'tipa' doesn't have to change the word after it.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Ешь давай.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Come on, eat.' 'Esh'' is the order 'eat!', for a friend. 'Davay' tacked on at the end makes it a nudge.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Я опоздаю на работу.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I will be late for work.' 'Opozdat na rabotu' is 'to be late onto work': 'na' with the 'u' ending, as when you go to work.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "mama",
   "t": "Да, кушайте быстрее.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Yes, eat faster.' 'Kushayte' is a cosy 'eat', with the 'te' that makes it polite and plural. 'Bystree' is 'faster'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Ладно, я съем это.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Alright, I will eat this.' 'Syem' is 'I will eat it up', and the 's' at the front adds the finishing. 'Ladno' is a grudging 'alright'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Это очень вкусно.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'This is very delicious.' 'Vkusno', tasty, comes from 'vkus', taste, and Russian needs no 'is'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "mama",
   "t": "Нам пора идти.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'It is time for us to go.' 'Nam pora', literally 'to us time', is 'it's time we went'. 'Idti' is to go on foot.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Спасибо за завтрак.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Thank you for the breakfast.' 'Spasibo za' plus the thing: 'za zavtrak'. 'Zavtrak' comes from the same root as 'zavtra', tomorrow; both are built on 'utro', morning. Now the breakfast scene, uncut.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
